WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.526
I've started on another book which I'm a

00:00:04.526 --> 00:00:07.826
couple hundred pages in, I guess. And then--

00:00:07.826 --> 00:00:09.986
Stewart: Was there a break between the two

00:00:09.986 --> 00:00:14.746
books at all? Or do you just never put down the pen?

00:00:14.746 --> 00:00:19.406
Neal: No... I tend to fall into a foul mood when

00:00:19.406 --> 00:00:21.296
I'm not doing something, when I'm not writing

00:00:21.296 --> 00:00:27.266
a book. And so I don't like to stop.

00:00:27.266 --> 00:00:29.366
And also I'm sort of... you know,

00:00:29.366 --> 00:00:32.276
putting the Long Now hat on for a second,

00:00:32.276 --> 00:00:35.936
I am aware that I will not live

00:00:35.936 --> 00:00:38.575
forever. And so, you know, hopefully

00:00:38.575 --> 00:00:41.516
I'll live for a while. But I wanted to break

00:00:41.516 --> 00:00:43.406
this habit of just stopping for a whole

00:00:43.406 --> 00:00:45.956
year between books.

00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:51.176
Stewart: Had you done that before?
Neal: Yeah pretty much.

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:52.756
So I just decided to keep going.

00:00:52.756 --> 00:00:55.976
I'm also working part-time for a company

00:00:55.976 --> 00:00:58.555
called Magic Leap which is making augmented

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:04.836
reality hardware of a novel type.

00:01:04.836 --> 00:01:07.856
And so that's also taking up a bunch of my

00:01:07.856 --> 00:01:10.856
time as well.
Stewart: Framing yourself into a

00:01:10.856 --> 00:01:13.196
novel, which is the sort of the

00:01:13.196 --> 00:01:17.696
original question... the idea for this one

00:01:17.696 --> 00:01:20.576
sort of percolated for a couple of

00:01:20.576 --> 00:01:24.996
decades... decade and a half.

00:01:24.996 --> 00:01:28.276
Is that usually the case that you've got

00:01:28.286 --> 00:01:30.986
a number of things out there percolating

00:01:30.986 --> 00:01:33.356
and... one sort of comes to life or...

00:01:33.356 --> 00:01:38.316
Neal: Pretty much, yeah, like the origin of Anathem

00:01:38.316 --> 00:01:42.316
was when Danny [Hillis] asked me

00:01:42.325 --> 00:01:44.536
and several other people to make

00:01:44.545 --> 00:01:46.856
back of napkin sketches of what the

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:49.466
[10,000 Year] Clock might look like. And that

00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:51.475
that would have been 01999 because he wanted it

00:01:51.475 --> 00:01:55.905
for a millennial web page. But I didn't start

00:01:55.905 --> 00:01:59.545
writing Anathem until 02005

00:01:59.545 --> 00:02:04.196
maybe? And then Reamde is based on a

00:02:04.196 --> 00:02:07.586
story of the Filipino hacker

00:02:07.586 --> 00:02:14.786
who made a virus in the early 2000s that

00:02:14.786 --> 00:02:18.176
went viral very very rapidly over the

00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:22.375
whole world. And so that was

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:25.196
probably another five to eight year

00:02:25.196 --> 00:02:28.436
gestation period. So yeah these things

00:02:28.436 --> 00:02:31.076
tend to kind of sit there and ferment in

00:02:31.076 --> 00:02:32.545
the background for quite a long time

00:02:32.545 --> 00:02:36.086
before, you know... it's kind of like insects

00:02:36.086 --> 00:02:39.446
that... you know spend a long time in the

00:02:39.446 --> 00:02:41.396
larval state and they come out and fly

00:02:41.396 --> 00:02:44.146
around for a little while.

00:02:44.146 --> 00:02:48.086
Stewart: there's one story that artists tell

00:02:48.086 --> 00:02:49.616
each other that may or may not relate

00:02:49.616 --> 00:02:51.265
here. I'm reading a wonderful memoir by

00:02:51.265 --> 00:02:53.696
Sally Mann, the photographer, called

00:02:53.696 --> 00:02:57.075
"Hold Still."  And in it she mentions that

00:02:57.075 --> 00:03:00.656
photographers she knows, and I guess other

00:03:00.656 --> 00:03:04.256
artists, who sort of have a big ambitious

00:03:04.256 --> 00:03:06.686
thing they're working on and they're

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.246
grinding away on it, they're grinding away on it...

00:03:08.246 --> 00:03:10.646
And it's very exciting at the

00:03:10.646 --> 00:03:12.536
beginning. It's all possibility and

00:03:12.536 --> 00:03:14.485
some of them are starting to play out

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:16.135
and you realize you've got something

00:03:16.135 --> 00:03:17.515
that's live and it's taking you

00:03:17.515 --> 00:03:19.105
somewhere and you're completely excited.

00:03:19.105 --> 00:03:21.056
But by the time you get to the end of

00:03:21.056 --> 00:03:24.436
the project... it's all punctuation. Neal: Yeah

00:03:24.436 --> 00:03:26.575
Stewart: ...grinding away and getting the last

00:03:26.575 --> 00:03:28.376
misspelling out of it or the print

00:03:28.376 --> 00:03:30.056
that's actually half a decent compared

00:03:30.056 --> 00:03:31.436
to what's in the negative all I kind of thing.

00:03:31.436 --> 00:03:33.595
And she said what she'd learned  from some

00:03:33.595 --> 00:03:35.696
other artists (like, I guess, [Cy Twombly])

00:03:35.696 --> 00:03:39.325
is that it's

00:03:39.325 --> 00:03:40.946
the healthy thing for an artist to do is

00:03:40.946 --> 00:03:44.006
as you get to that tiresome end game...

00:03:44.006 --> 00:03:46.226
have something else starting to crank up that

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:48.325
is already getting you excited again.

00:03:48.325 --> 00:03:50.396
Neal: Yeah. Stewart: ...so you don't have to spend

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.765
a whole lot of time on the: 'I can't believe

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.705
this is dragging on so long, can I just stop it,

00:03:55.705 --> 00:03:57.716
I hate myself'... all that. You can go on to a

00:03:57.716 --> 00:04:00.235
new mode of excitement. And then in your

00:04:00.235 --> 00:04:02.515
spare time finish the punctuation on the

00:04:02.515 --> 00:04:03.716
previous work. Is that what you're

00:04:03.716 --> 00:04:05.575
finding this time around?  Neal: Well it's

00:04:05.575 --> 00:04:08.786
always been the case. And it used to be

00:04:08.786 --> 00:04:11.426
I would just get so distracted by

00:04:11.426 --> 00:04:15.295
the punctuation, literally, that I wouldn't

00:04:15.295 --> 00:04:18.686
even try to start something. But you know

00:04:18.686 --> 00:04:21.686
the more i do this the more I realize that

00:04:21.686 --> 00:04:27.086
I can get bits of productive time out.

00:04:27.086 --> 00:04:31.560
If I kind of budget my time well.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.480
Yeah, I'm just trying to waste as little

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.200
time as possible. Stewart: Here here!

